This page is not a handwritten note. It is a typed page, page 118 of Exposé IX of SGA 7 I (Grothendieck, 'Modèles de Néron et monodromie', LNM 288, 1972). The bottom stamp '436' is consistent with LNM 288 pagination. It sits among the handwritten 1975-76 notes on divided-power de Rham complexes (previous and next pages), presumably as scrap paper or by misfiling, and is unrelated to them.

Setting: K is the fraction field of a henselian DVR with residue characteristic p, A an abelian variety over K with dual A', and A^o, A'^o the identity components of the special fibres of their Néron models. T_ℓ(A^o)^t ⊂ T_ℓ(A^o)^f are the toric part and the 'fixed' (finite) part of the ℓ-adic Tate module, viewed as pro-ℓ group schemes. M, M' are the character groups of the toric parts, with M_ℓ = M⊗Z_ℓ.

The page first ends the remark in 9.5. The homomorphism (9.5.6), built from a double extension E/F/R/P/Q, gives the canonical action of inertia I on X_η(K̄) as defined in 9.2. One checks this by reducing to 9.4 with n a power of ℓ and all modules locally constant.

9.6 then defines the canonical pairing (9.1.2) on the groups of connected components, Φ_A × Φ_{A'} → Q/Z, for every ℓ including ℓ = p. For ℓ ≠ p it must agree with the Galois-theoretic (monodromy) definition of 9.2. The 9.5 machinery is applied to:
- P = T_ℓ(A^o)^t ≅ M̌'_ℓ(1);
- Q = M_ℓ;
- R = T_ℓ(A^o)^f / T_ℓ(A^o)^t;
- F = T_ℓ(A^o)^f;
- E = D(F') = D(T_ℓ(A'^o)^f).

Here D is Cartier duality for pro-ℓ Barsotti–Tate systems, taken levelwise and in the limit. F is an extension of R by P. Dually, F' is an extension of R' by P' = M̌_ℓ(1). Cartier duality therefore makes E an extension of D(P') = M_ℓ by D(R'). Using the duality D(R') ≅ R of the abelian parts (5.5, proved in 7.4.6), E becomes an extension of Q by R. This is exactly the input that 9.5 needs to produce the pairing.
